WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.042 --> 00:00:08.250
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: So what
are we going to learn today?

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.740
We're going to talk
about the three

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.440
products that make up APM--

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.970
Stackdriver Trace,
Stackdriver Debugger,

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:15.260
Stackdriver Profiler--

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.373
with a high-level demo,
perhaps an announcement.

00:00:17.373 --> 00:00:19.040
And then Nejc's going
to come up to talk

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.410
about how he uses
this at Outfit7

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:25.040
to make meaningful changes.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:26.882
So before we get
started, I just want

00:00:26.882 --> 00:00:28.590
to learn a little bit
about the audience.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.980
So how many of you guys
consider yourselves developers,

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.990
have a developer title?

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:36.290
And how many more on
the operator side?

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:39.410
How many are both?

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:42.530
And how many of those
hold the title SRE,

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.700
or something similar to that?

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:45.500
Awesome.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:46.460
Good bunch.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.500
Now as far as
Stackdriver goes, who's

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.990
familiar with Stackdriver?

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:54.360
And who uses Stackdriver
in production today?

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:55.100
Awesome, great.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.950
So we've got a lot of usage, so
we'll make this really quick.

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:00.270
But for those who didn't
have their hand up,

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:03.200
Stackdriver is Google
Cloud's observability suite.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.900
So we try to make Google the
most observable cloud as much

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.318
as possible and help you
learn more about what's

00:01:08.318 --> 00:01:10.610
happening your environment,
so you can resolve problems

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.820
quicker and make meaningful
performance changes,

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.430
reduce cost, and
just, overall, help

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.280
to run the best system
you can with the best

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.990
possible information.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.900
And our job is really to help
you guys help your customers

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.710
to make that a reality.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.610
So the Stackdriver portfolio
consists of four main tools,

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:30.100
or main groups of tools.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:33.470
There's monitoring,
logging, APM, and IRM.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:34.400
IRM is our newest--

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:35.960
Incident Response Management.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.520
We're talking about
APM today, which

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.190
is Application
Performance Monitoring,

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:43.310
and it's meant to be used in
context of everything else.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.830
APM gives you part of the
story, that deeper level

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:47.490
of debug information.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:50.720
However, we do want to make
sure that you also have that

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.840
paired with metrics for good,
statistically relevant data

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.180
and logs for that more
traditional, troubleshooting

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.770
style information.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.630
So from a high-level
perspective,

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.240
let's talk about APM.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.450
As I mentioned, there are
three products in the suite--

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.660
Trace, Profiler, and Debugger.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.030
Trace is our distributed
tracing product.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.310
Now, who here has heard
of distributed tracing?

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:14.480
Who's familiar?

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.330
Probably the same people who put
up their hands for Stackdriver.

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.730
And how many people
are using distributed

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:21.960
tracing in production today?

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:22.610
Awesome.

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:26.150
And keep your hand up if you're
using Stackdriver's Trace?

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:27.980
OK, cool.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.780
So for those who didn't
have their hand up,

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.450
distributed tracing helps you
to visualize the request as it

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:33.817
flows through your environment.

00:02:33.817 --> 00:02:35.900
This is especially important
in microservice-based

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:36.923
applications.

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:38.840
And the goal is to help
you determine the root

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:42.650
cause faster, analyze trends,
and understand your topology

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:44.837
and how your services
call each other

00:02:44.837 --> 00:02:46.670
and, ultimately, to
make meaningful changes,

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:48.260
as I mentioned before.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:49.940
So to illustrate this,
here's an example

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.400
of what an application
architecture could look like.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.160
You have your users connecting
to your load balancer, which

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.220
sends traffic to the front end,
multiple back end services,

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.140
perhaps compartmentalized.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.050
Not monolithic applications,
as things once were.

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:06.080
And that communicates
to multiple back

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.420
end platform services,
such as databases,

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.150
analytics layers, data
warehouses, et cetera.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.310
However, today things
look a little bit more

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:15.595
like this, where things
are more chaotic,

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:16.970
and we're talking
about hundreds,

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:19.060
if not thousands, of
small microservices

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:21.320
is working together
for a common goal.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:25.010
And this is where tracing comes
in, shining a light on the path

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:26.980
that your requests take
through the system,

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:28.880
so that you can
troubleshoot problems

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.020
a heck of a lot easier.

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:32.000
If you can only see
one very small part

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.100
of an overall picture,
it's quite difficult

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:35.850
to understand the root
cause of a problem.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.660
Context matters, flow
matters, and tracing

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:42.302
helps you understand
that full path.

00:03:42.302 --> 00:03:43.760
So without further
ado, we're going

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.610
to do a brief demo
of Stackdriver Trace,

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:51.020
and we'll start here
on the Overview tab.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:52.840
So here in the
Overview tab, you can

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:57.190
understand your most common
requests-- your URIs, your RPC

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:59.290
calls, and their
associate latency,

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:04.600
plus a snapshot of your
last analysis report.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.730
But most the magic is actually
in the Trace Waterfall view.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:09.730
Now, for those of you
familiar with Stackdriver,

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.140
you'll see that this UI
looks a little bit different.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:13.510
This is actually something
that we've recently

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:14.500
launched to Alpha.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.120
If you're interested,
reach out to me,

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.079
bryanzimmerman@google.com.

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:19.500
Bryan with a y.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.097
We'd love to have you join.

00:04:21.097 --> 00:04:22.930
It looks a little bit
different in the past,

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:25.510
but the goal is still the same,
that you can use these filter

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:28.330
terms to really find
the trace that you're

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:31.060
trying to find that helps you
identify the problem you're

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.507
trying to troubleshoot.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.670
Now, once you find a trace, you
simply select it from the list,

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:42.390
and you can see it pop up
in the Waterfall View below.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:45.810
So each of these bars
are basically a span--

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.300
sorry, they're what
we call a span,

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.250
and a span is effectively
a call to a service.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:53.390
So the route span encompasses
the entire request,

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:56.330
and every child span
is a downstream service

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:59.960
that that request calls, so
you can understand that flow.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.680
Here, we've got an overall
request that's quite long.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.330
There's lots of calls.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:07.740
And a lot of them, if you
look, are very similar.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.775
It's calling get product, which
is calling get product again.

00:05:10.775 --> 00:05:12.150
There's a lot of
repetition here.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:13.620
We're not quite sure why.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.490
Off to the right-hand side,
it's even more apparent

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.020
that you have an
overall trace time

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:21.500
of 31,000 milliseconds,
which is obviously not

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.240
acceptable by any user.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:26.930
But 29,000 milliseconds
of that total

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:30.870
is actually caused by these
14 calls to the get product,

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.900
so this is obviously
the cause of the problem

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:34.670
and what we need
to troubleshoot.

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:36.560
And you can understand
how this might

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.310
be difficult to surmise
without a product like Trace.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:43.670
Each one of these calls
isn't by itself wrong

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:45.420
and isn't by itself
horribly long.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.820
But when you put them
together, their overall impact

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.420
to the whole is substantial
and really the root cause

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:53.540
of what is a significant
user-facing issue.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:57.200
Once you identify which is the
service you're interested in,

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.530
you can click on it to get
more contextual information,

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:03.080
correlate with log data,
look at event data.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.960
And you can actually send
a custom timed or not

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:09.530
timed annotations that
show up in the service.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:12.650
Now, while we're on the
subject, from an instrumentation

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:15.470
perspective, we actually
recommend you adopt OpenCensus.

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:17.450
My colleague, Morgan,
has a talk on Thursday

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:18.820
to talk about that exhaustively.

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:20.570
So we won't have time
to go into it today,

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:23.510
but that's a talk that I
would definitely attend.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:26.900
Lots of great information.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:28.400
The analysis reports
gives you more

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:31.950
of an understanding of things
from a trend perspective.

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:36.110
So in addition to understanding
that particular trace,

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:37.760
that one little
request, if I want

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.810
to understand what's happening
overall with my service, what

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:43.910
has changed in a major or minor
way that might be affecting

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:46.190
my users' experience, even
if it's not immediately

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:47.780
apparent to me,
the analysis report

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:50.220
does the math to bring
it to the surface.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.680
And those can either be done
on-demand or automatically.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.140
In this particular
example report,

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:57.020
we can see that the
high end 95th percentile

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:58.910
latency is great,
but for some reason,

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:01.160
my 50th percentile
latency, which

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:04.220
is the general case
for performance,

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:06.440
or the general case for
my users' experience

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:10.020
is double what it used to be,
so that's obviously a problem,

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.700
And you can see that in the
percentile groupings below.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:15.650
And you can actually even
drill into example traces

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:17.810
to understand what
exactly is happening

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:19.760
in that instance
for that report.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.740
And that gives you a
high-level view of Trace,

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:28.233
and we're going to move to
the next product in our suite.

00:07:28.233 --> 00:07:30.150
So how these fit together
is they are designed

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:31.920
to work as a team, of course.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:34.110
Trace helps you get
from problem to service.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:35.970
Profiler, which Morgan
will talk about,

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:37.800
helps you get from
service to method.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.840
And Debugger, which
is the third product,

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.600
helps you get from method
to the actual problem

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:43.690
that you're seeing.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:45.610
And it does this by
allowing you to take

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:49.470
a debug information on the fly.

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:53.250
So without a tool like
this, if I have a problem,

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:57.090
I might look at the logs, try
to understand what's happening,

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:59.670
stare at the code a bit,
come up with a theory.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.610
And maybe I have the log lines
that I need, but more likely,

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:06.330
I have to add a debug module
for some additional logging.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.663
Then I have to deploy that,
perhaps restart the service.

00:08:08.663 --> 00:08:10.247
Then I'm going to
wait for the problem

00:08:10.247 --> 00:08:11.850
to happen again,
search the logs,

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:15.110
and repeat on and on, until
I understand the problem.

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:16.980
Well, with Debugger,
because you can

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.380
take snapshots to
inspect local variables

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:23.610
and add log lines in production
safely without restarting,

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:25.680
without redeploying,
you can tighten

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:28.200
that troubleshooting
flow quite a bit.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.558
Your lengthy deploys
are out of the equation,

00:08:30.558 --> 00:08:32.850
and that makes it quicker
for you to get to resolution,

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:34.590
affecting customer impact.

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:38.950
And let's see what this
looks like in real life.

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:41.610
So we're going to open
that same application,

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:44.520
and we're going to go
to an example log line.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.180
Taking a snapshot is as
simple as clicking on the line

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:49.320
I'm interested in.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.750
See on the right-hand side
it's waiting for that snapshot

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:52.620
to be hit.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:55.013
The next time that piece
of code is executed,

00:08:55.013 --> 00:08:56.430
it will gather all
the information

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.163
about the local
variables at that time,

00:08:58.163 --> 00:09:00.330
to the limit of what's
reasonable from a performance

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:01.170
impact perspective.

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:06.540
Most of the magic of this is
actually in making sure that it

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:08.820
does not impact production,
making sure that

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:10.980
your conditions-- which
you can add, by the way,

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:14.400
you can have this snapshot
only taken when you need--

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:18.180
or the expressions to print
certain variables, et cetera,

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:20.130
aren't going to impact
your running code.

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.050
And then it's run in
a sandbox to make sure

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:25.590
that it's not actually going
to impact from a resource

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:29.010
perspective or affect the
bytecode, as I mentioned.

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:32.020
The actual snapshot is over
here on the right-hand side,

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:36.070
and we can walk the
call stack, look

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:39.170
through the local variables at
any that have been captured.

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:40.810
If the one you want
isn't captured,

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:42.820
you can add that to the
expression, no problem.

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:45.360
And that really gives you
that deep-level information

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.377
of, OK, well, what's happening
in production right now?

00:09:48.377 --> 00:09:49.960
So if you've ever
troubleshot an issue

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:56.458
that you could not reproduce
in QA, Debugger's for you.

00:09:56.458 --> 00:09:58.750
If you've ever forgotten to
add a line of log statement

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:00.740
that you wish you had,
Debugger's for you.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:04.330
So these are the kind of
problems we try to solve

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:05.920
And with that being
said, I'm going

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:07.150
to hand things over to
my colleague, Morgan,

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:08.108
to talk about Profiler.

00:10:10.612 --> 00:10:11.820
MORGAN MCLEAN: Thanks, Bryan.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.107
So if you attended
Cloud Next last year,

00:10:17.107 --> 00:10:19.440
you probably would've seen
that we announced Stackdriver

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:22.500
Profiler;s public beta on
stage at the keynote and during

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:24.270
the APM breakout.

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:26.550
Since then, we've had a
ton of customer success

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:27.450
stories of Profiler.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:28.862
I'll get into those in a minute.

00:10:28.862 --> 00:10:30.570
But we're really,
really proud of how far

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:31.950
the product has come.

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:33.780
For those who aren't
already aware,

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:36.180
Stackdriver Profiler
is a production code

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:37.770
performance analysis tool.

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:39.990
It runs continually on your
production applications,

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:42.060
with effectively no
performance impact,

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:44.280
and allows you to dive
into code all the way down

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:47.130
to the function level to see
where your code is being slow

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:49.770
and where it's
costing you resources.

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:52.050
Profiler, today, works
on applications that

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:55.230
use Java, Node, Go, and Python.

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:57.000
We recently added
Python support.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.610
And as of today, Profiler
is now generally available,

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:02.020
which is what we're
really excited about.

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:04.950
Now talk is cheap, and
it's difficult to describe

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:06.658
some of these products
from a high level,

00:11:06.658 --> 00:11:08.492
so I'm going to dive
into a demo to show you

00:11:08.492 --> 00:11:10.500
how we can use Profiler
to debug performance

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:13.580
problems on a running
production application.

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:14.730
Perfect.

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:19.470
So this is one of the instances
of the Hipster Shop demo

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:22.008
that you've probably seen
in many, many Google talks.

00:11:22.008 --> 00:11:23.425
It's a microservices
application--

00:11:23.425 --> 00:11:26.007
I think it's the same one that
Bryan was using in his demo--

00:11:26.007 --> 00:11:27.840
with a whole bunch of
services inside of it,

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:29.340
written in different languages.

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:32.610
Right now, we're using Profiler
to inspect the product catalog

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:33.420
service.

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:35.820
The service, I think,
is written in Go,

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:37.500
and it's the
database of products

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:40.230
inside this fake
e-commerce application.

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:43.680
So to start, Profiler aggregates
your performance data down

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:44.437
by the service.

00:11:44.437 --> 00:11:46.020
This product catalog
service, I think,

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:47.102
has about five instances.

00:11:47.102 --> 00:11:48.810
We're viewing the
performance information

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:49.845
across all of those.

00:11:49.845 --> 00:11:51.720
As I mentioned, this is
running in production

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:53.880
with effectively no
performance impact, which

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:56.220
is what separates Stackdriver
Profiler from most

00:11:56.220 --> 00:11:57.780
other profiling tools.

00:11:57.780 --> 00:11:58.615
It runs continually.

00:11:58.615 --> 00:12:00.240
You don't turn it on
for a brief period

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:01.530
and then turn it off again.

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:02.940
You don't have to turn
it on and freak out

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:05.023
that it's going to take
down your system by adding

00:12:05.023 --> 00:12:05.850
extra load.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:06.820
It's very efficient.

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:08.760
It's very intuitive.

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:13.320
Profiler can inspect the
performance of your code

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:15.970
across CPU performance,
memory performance,

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:17.370
and thread contention.

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:19.227
Today, we're going
to look at CPU.

00:12:19.227 --> 00:12:21.060
You can, of course,
break down your profiles

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:23.110
across zones or different
versions of your app.

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:25.130
We're not going to do that
today, but it's available,

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:27.463
and there's some other filters
I'll gets to in a minute.

00:12:27.463 --> 00:12:28.810
By default this is what you see.

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:29.790
This is a flame graph.

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:31.410
If you've used other
profiling tools,

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:33.750
you're probably
familiar with this.

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.280
On the flame graph, on
the horizontal axis,

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:38.550
we're showing the performance
cost of every function.

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:40.320
Each box that you see
on the flame graph

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:43.290
represents a function
in your code base.

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:46.410
And so, functions that are
taking more horizontal space--

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:48.132
so like the one I mousing over--

00:12:48.132 --> 00:12:49.590
take up more,
basically costing you

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:51.420
more in terms of
performance and money.

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:54.070
On the vertical axis, we're
showing you the call tree.

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:57.150
So in this case, we've got
streams that 1.1, that calls

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:59.700
handles stream, the calls
process [? UnerRPC, ?]

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:01.320
and so on.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:03.450
And so right away, we
can tell that we're

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:06.937
burning about half our CPU on
this big stack on the left,

00:13:06.937 --> 00:13:08.520
and if we dive into
it, we'll discover

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:11.360
that this is related to JSON
serialization, deserialization.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:13.860
Probably not something you want
to be spending a lot of time

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:16.418
on a catalog service that's
effectively a database.

00:13:16.418 --> 00:13:18.210
We'll come back to the
in-depth performance

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:19.502
of that in a minute.

00:13:19.502 --> 00:13:21.210
So flame graphs are
great for finding out

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:23.850
problems where you have like one
big function in your code base

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:26.280
or one big stack that's
consuming a lot of resources.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:28.350
And yeah, we've had a
lot of customers who've

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:31.740
had gotchas like these, but
Profiler's a lot more powerful

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:33.020
than just finding that.

00:13:33.020 --> 00:13:35.910
What I'm going to open next is
what we call the Focus Table.

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:39.660
The Focus Table shows us where
we're burning CPUs, aggregated

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:41.280
across all functions.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:44.370
You can sort this
by self-consumption.

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:47.000
Self-consumption of a
function is the amount of CPU

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.050
or memory burned
by that function,

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:50.430
but not by its children.

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:52.980
Or you can sort it by
the total consumption.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:55.620
In this case, we're
going to zoom in here

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:57.150
on the state and
string call, which

00:13:57.150 --> 00:14:00.840
is burning over 5% of our CPU.

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:02.460
This brings us to
the Focus View.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:05.857
So Profiler isn't just useful
for finding the performance

00:14:05.857 --> 00:14:06.690
impact of your code.

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:09.990
It's also useful for finding how
your code actually calls itself

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:11.040
in production.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:14.430
This Focus View has taken every
instance of state and string

00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:17.100
in our application, and it's
showing us the different ways

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:20.880
that we can get there from root
and how the performance cost

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:22.610
of each of those call paths.

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:24.360
This particular case--
it's not telling us

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:25.770
anything really interesting.

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:27.630
What is interesting
about Focus is

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:30.640
that it will also show you all
the ways out of that function.

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:32.760
And we can observe
that there are none,

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:35.723
so there's no other functions
listed under state and string.

00:14:35.723 --> 00:14:37.140
That tells us that
if we're tasked

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:39.500
with improving the
performance of the service,

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.680
state and string is a
good place to start,

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:43.740
because it's
burning its own CPU.

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:45.780
It's burning over 5%
of the CPU aggregated

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:47.667
across the entire system.

00:14:47.667 --> 00:14:50.250
And finally, it's not it's not
spending that time in its child

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:50.610
functions.

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:52.770
If you want optimize it, you
can optimize that function

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:53.270
on its own.

00:14:58.668 --> 00:15:00.210
One of the other
features of Profiler

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:02.547
that we've had since
the start is coloring.

00:15:02.547 --> 00:15:05.130
A lot of our customers have told
us how aesthetically pleasing

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:07.908
Profiler is, but then when we
ran a survey of our customer

00:15:07.908 --> 00:15:10.450
base and asked them what they
thought the colors represented,

00:15:10.450 --> 00:15:12.450
they all told us
they had no idea.

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:15.210
This gave us some data that we
should probably change things.

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:16.950
So starting one
or two weeks ago,

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:19.380
we added different
coloring modes to Profiler.

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:21.240
I'll show you those now.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:24.990
Probably the most useful is the
total cost coloring mode, which

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:26.190
generally colors the graph--

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:29.700
excuse me-- recolors
the graph, so you

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:31.980
can see where you're
burning CPU more visually.

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:33.638
This is a bit of
an obvious example.

00:15:33.638 --> 00:15:35.430
These functions were
already taking up half

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:36.540
of the horizontal
space, but there

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:37.915
are a lot of
situations where you

00:15:37.915 --> 00:15:39.950
can use this, like the
Focus Table, to find

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:42.060
and code that's burning
CPU, and it's sprinkled

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:43.143
throughout your code base.

00:15:47.337 --> 00:15:48.920
Finally, one of the
really interesting

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:51.360
features we have is
called weighted filtering.

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:54.530
So this example is an
app that, in its best

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:57.530
case or its average case,
has this JSON deserialization

00:15:57.530 --> 00:15:59.510
function that's consuming
about half its CPU.

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:02.430
That's already egregious,
just to be clear.

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:06.740
But you can get many workloads
where the usage or consumption

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:10.400
is somewhat bursty where,
during normal periods,

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:14.120
you know, nothing looks like
it's out of whack or that it's

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:14.900
weird.

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:17.960
But you'll have periods where
your system's under higher

00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:20.270
stress, there's some kind of
background process running

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:23.210
or something else where,
suddenly, the characterization

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:25.550
of the CPU performance is
dramatically different.

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:28.070
Because Profiler's an
aggregate analysis tool,

00:16:28.070 --> 00:16:30.710
it generally wouldn't be good
at capturing those, which is why

00:16:30.710 --> 00:16:32.678
we added weighted filtering.

00:16:32.678 --> 00:16:34.220
So what weighted
filtering lets me do

00:16:34.220 --> 00:16:37.730
is analyze the profiles from
periods of peak consumption.

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:39.410
What I'm going to
do here is jump

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:42.470
to the CPU profiles
captured when the CPU was

00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:44.810
at its top 5% of utilization.

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:47.630
Right here, we can see that that
parse catalog function, that's

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:50.750
burning our CPU and all of
the deep JSON deserialization

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:53.180
functions underneath
it, are now consuming

00:16:53.180 --> 00:16:56.330
over 90% of our system's
consumption, as opposed

00:16:56.330 --> 00:16:59.270
to the average case
that was close to 50%.

00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:01.680
And in fact, we can even
do a nice comparison here,

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:05.900
where we can compare the weight
of the top 5% to the average.

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:08.630
And right away, we can see
that this new coloring mode

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:11.240
that, yes, that's, in fact,
exactly where the increase

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:13.339
in CPU consumption has gone.

00:17:13.339 --> 00:17:15.140
So Profiler is
great for improving

00:17:15.140 --> 00:17:16.640
the performance of
your application,

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:17.720
like we've done here.

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:20.150
It's also a very powerful
cost-reduction tool.

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:22.490
Some of our largest
Google Cloud customers

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:25.099
have reduced their aggregate,
like their fleet-wide CPU

00:17:25.099 --> 00:17:26.683
spend, by over
10% with Profiler,

00:17:26.683 --> 00:17:28.850
and there's some who have
achieved even bigger gains

00:17:28.850 --> 00:17:30.508
in the 20% to 30% range.

00:17:30.508 --> 00:17:31.550
It's incredibly powerful.

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:33.710
It's something we use
inside of Google across most

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:37.250
of our services all the time,
to both save money and optimize

00:17:37.250 --> 00:17:38.677
the performance of our code.

00:17:38.677 --> 00:17:40.760
As I mentioned before,
Profiler has been available

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:42.950
as a beta for the
last year or so.

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:46.020
It is now generally available,
is fully supported by Google,

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:48.020
and we're just really,
really happy to see where

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:49.760
we're going to take this next.

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:52.640
To that end, I'm going to
invite Njec from Outfit7

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:55.790
to come on stage and show us
how Outfit7 has used Profiler

00:17:55.790 --> 00:17:58.057
to reduce their costs
and dramatically improve

00:17:58.057 --> 00:17:59.640
the performance of
their applications.

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:00.305
Thank you.

00:18:00.305 --> 00:18:01.430
NEJC SEVER: Thanks, Morgan.

00:18:04.030 --> 00:18:05.240
Hi, everyone.

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:07.690
My name is Nejc, and I'm a
senior software engineer,

00:18:07.690 --> 00:18:12.850
working in back end development
team for Outfit7 mobile games.

00:18:12.850 --> 00:18:14.800
And today, let's
all imagine that we

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:16.870
want to improve our
code performance

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:19.390
and make our
operational costs lower.

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:22.600
And I'm going to
share with you how

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:26.530
we do this at Outfit7
using App Engine Standard,

00:18:26.530 --> 00:18:30.500
and how Profiler makes that
process easier for you.

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:34.623
But first, let me get to know
you with Outfit7 and my friend

00:18:34.623 --> 00:18:37.040
here, who will be joining me
throughout this presentation,

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:38.500
Talking Tom.

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:42.310
So for Outfit7, it all
started back in 2010,

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:44.470
with a game called
"Talking Tom."

00:18:44.470 --> 00:18:46.450
And at that time,
"Talking Tom" turned out

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:48.880
to be a worldwide phenomenon.

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:51.840
And from that funny
mobile game, Outfit7

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:53.830
grew to be one of the
fastest-growing family

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:56.380
entertainment companies
on the planet,

00:18:56.380 --> 00:18:58.330
and is now the fourth
biggest publisher

00:18:58.330 --> 00:19:02.140
of mobile games by downloads.

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:05.620
So at Outfit7, it's all
about good and honest fun,

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:08.930
and that's also the main reason
why "Talking Tom and Friends"

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:11.050
franchise is known
and loved by fans

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:14.150
of all ages and generations.

00:19:14.150 --> 00:19:16.120
Now, did you know
that on average,

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:18.670
every single one of you has
downloaded one of our apps

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:19.880
before?

00:19:19.880 --> 00:19:23.740
And that's because we have
more than 9 billion downloads.

00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:26.650
And you can go to any
favorite mobile app store

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:32.560
and download one of our 21
free-to-play mobile games,

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:35.020
ranging from top
Tamagotchi games, runner

00:19:35.020 --> 00:19:37.750
games, or other
genres, and we also

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:39.760
have some multi-player games.

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:44.410
We have around 350 million
monthly active users.

00:19:44.410 --> 00:19:46.210
Jus to put that
number in perspective,

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:48.690
it's like every US
citizen would play

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:51.820
our apps on a regular basis.

00:19:51.820 --> 00:19:53.440
But as you might
know, great looking

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:56.830
games are not enough to deliver
all this fun through such

00:19:56.830 --> 00:19:59.650
a huge fan base
like Outfit7 has.

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:02.200
Behind the scenes, we
ran complex systems,

00:20:02.200 --> 00:20:05.560
like real-time game servers
and analytic pipelines

00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:07.510
that need to scale massively.

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:10.120
But today, I would
like to be focusing

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:13.180
on the backbone between client
devices and our back end

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:15.100
servers.

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:19.440
So our main mobile back end
runs on App Engine Standard.

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:22.470
And we are on App Engine
Standard since the beginning

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:26.340
because it enables you to focus
mainly on application code,

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:31.200
while the infrastructure is
automatically managed for you.

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:34.980
We run more than 10
App Engine services.

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:38.170
For our main database, we
are using Cloud Datastore,

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:41.700
and for orchestration tasks,
we are using Cloud Tasks.

00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:45.630
So let's see how we
profiled this app before.

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:49.020
First, we needed to set up
a local development server.

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:51.900
This was quite an easy
step because it comes out

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:57.660
of App Engine SDK by default,
so that was quite an easy step.

00:20:57.660 --> 00:21:01.200
Then, we needed to define
different profiling scenarios.

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:04.590
And by this, I mean different
application entry points

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:07.470
where your code needs
to perform well.

00:21:07.470 --> 00:21:09.540
And this step is
really important

00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:13.140
because different entry
points to your application

00:21:13.140 --> 00:21:15.630
can lead to totally
different execution paths,

00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:17.520
and those different
execution paths

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:21.190
target different
aspects of your code.

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:24.930
And here, we also have to
consider both requests coming

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:27.450
from client devices and
those from backgrounds

00:21:27.450 --> 00:21:29.760
running processes.

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:33.420
Then, we needed to populate
some production-like data

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:35.610
to our local database.

00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:39.430
We did this through some helper
scripts for data population.

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:42.060
But as you might
know, as time goes on

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:45.450
and application code changes,
so do data store entities,

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:49.200
and you have to manage
both application

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:51.970
codes and those scripts
that populate the data.

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:56.310
Otherwise, you can
do profiling easy.

00:21:56.310 --> 00:21:59.770
So we had our
production-like environments

00:21:59.770 --> 00:22:02.040
set up on our local
development server.

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:05.310
Now, we need to generate
some traffic to it, in order

00:22:05.310 --> 00:22:09.510
to get some sampling data
for the profiling stage.

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:13.040
Luckily for us, we have a
great integration testing team

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:15.870
that has most of our
use cases covered

00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:19.650
and can generate quite
a good load for us

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:22.660
to be able to profile
our application.

00:22:22.660 --> 00:22:25.020
So the last step was
to attach the Profiler,

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:28.500
some third-party profiler, to
the running local development

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:31.810
server and analyze the results.

00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:35.050
So as you can see, this
process was really tedious,

00:22:35.050 --> 00:22:38.650
and there was little
to no automation to it.

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:41.160
And at the end, we could
still make some use cases

00:22:41.160 --> 00:22:44.310
because it was just an imitation
of the production environment,

00:22:44.310 --> 00:22:47.190
and it wasn't actually
running on production.

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:52.090
So Stackdriver Profiler made
that process simpler for us.

00:22:52.090 --> 00:22:54.480
It gets rid of all of
the necessary steps

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:57.600
that we needed to take before,
and all we are left to do

00:22:57.600 --> 00:23:00.630
is to attach Stackdriver
Profiler to our App Engine

00:23:00.630 --> 00:23:01.830
application.

00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:05.190
And that is as easy as inserting
one line into App Engine

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:08.590
configuration and
applying it to production.

00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:11.700
So our local development server
gets replaced with a production

00:23:11.700 --> 00:23:12.750
server.

00:23:12.750 --> 00:23:15.960
All the data and traffic
is running on production,

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:19.300
so there is no limitation of
the production environment.

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:21.150
And as you can see,
this process is so easy

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:23.580
that it also makes
our Talking Tom,

00:23:23.580 --> 00:23:26.760
our friend here, a
little bit happier.

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:29.970
So using Stackdriver
Profiler we are already

00:23:29.970 --> 00:23:33.940
able to get rid of some of
our other memory exceptions.

00:23:33.940 --> 00:23:38.250
Those, quite often, cause some
of our batch processing jobs

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:39.510
to fail.

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:42.210
And using the
Profiler's dashboard,

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:48.090
we were able to spot the
high-memory consuming functions

00:23:48.090 --> 00:23:50.040
and split them
into multiple parts

00:23:50.040 --> 00:23:52.380
and got rid of the problem.

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:54.600
We also did some
performance checks,

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:58.440
and we reduced the CPU usage on
some of our App Engine services

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:07.650
to around 15%, which also made
our operational costs lower.

00:24:07.650 --> 00:24:10.410
But one interesting case that
I would like to point out

00:24:10.410 --> 00:24:14.110
today was of some
bad afternoon deploy.

00:24:14.110 --> 00:24:16.290
So here's the story.

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:19.290
We had some late
afternoon deploy

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:22.020
with the code that
patched old code

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:26.280
reviews, unit test, integration
test, and staging environment.

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:29.910
And at the end, it still
triggered our alerting system,

00:24:29.910 --> 00:24:32.970
and an alert was indicated
that we had an increased

00:24:32.970 --> 00:24:35.820
number of instances running
on some of our App Engine

00:24:35.820 --> 00:24:37.590
services.

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:41.350
So we went quickly through
code change history,

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:44.520
but we were unable
to spot any suspects

00:24:44.520 --> 00:24:46.470
because the change
in the code was

00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:50.070
so subtle that at first glimpse
it didn't affect performance

00:24:50.070 --> 00:24:52.050
at all.

00:24:52.050 --> 00:24:55.530
So using-- before the
Stackdriver Profiler,

00:24:55.530 --> 00:24:57.750
we would either revert
the full deploy, which

00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:00.210
would postpone our
deployed features,

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:03.450
or we would postpone the
investigation of the problem

00:25:03.450 --> 00:25:06.480
until the next day, which
means our costs would increase,

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:11.980
because we would run multiple
App Engine instances.

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:14.790
But with Stackdriver
Profiler, using

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:19.650
the feature when you can compare
two versions to each other,

00:25:19.650 --> 00:25:21.080
we compared--

00:25:21.080 --> 00:25:23.820
we split 50% of the
production graph traffic

00:25:23.820 --> 00:25:28.250
to the latest version and to
the one before the broken one.

00:25:28.250 --> 00:25:30.300
And Stackdriver
Profiler dashboards

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:35.490
has this neat feature when
you're in comparison mode--

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:38.730
it highlight the functions that
are performing better or worse

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:40.980
with three different colors.

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:43.890
And using this feature, we
were able to quickly pinpoint

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:49.050
the problem and got rid of it
in a matter of half an hour.

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:52.890
So what's the greatest
Profiler value for us?

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:54.870
The fact that it's
so easy to deploy

00:25:54.870 --> 00:25:57.550
and that it runs on
production environment--

00:25:57.550 --> 00:26:00.390
this opened quite a few
opportunities for us.

00:26:00.390 --> 00:26:03.420
We are now able to
do code performance

00:26:03.420 --> 00:26:07.860
checks on a regular basis, which
means our code runs smoother,

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:12.750
requires less resources to run,
and it also affects our cost

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:14.800
in a positive way.

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:18.060
We also have great
insights to our application

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:20.670
because, before,
App Engine Standard

00:26:20.670 --> 00:26:23.360
was really a black box for us.

00:26:23.360 --> 00:26:26.280
And the last point that
I would like to make

00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:31.110
is Stackdriver Profiler is also
now a tool to react to alerts.

00:26:31.110 --> 00:26:34.260
So for example, if
we to get an alert

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:35.880
for increased
number of instances

00:26:35.880 --> 00:26:38.010
running on App
Engine, this means

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:39.900
that our costs will be higher.

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:42.870
If we get an alert
for increased latency,

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:45.000
this means bad user experience.

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:47.940
And now, if we get
these alerts, we

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:51.000
have new tool to check if
the cause of those alerts

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:53.130
is in bad performing
code, which we

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:57.900
weren't able to do that before,
at least not that quickly.

00:26:57.900 --> 00:27:00.570
So I hope that in this
short presentation,

00:27:00.570 --> 00:27:03.570
by me showing you how Outfit7
manages code performance,

00:27:03.570 --> 00:27:05.640
you gained some
knowledge, and you

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:09.660
can apply it next time that
your code performance suffers.

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:10.936
Thanks.

